    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, find A, the number of four-digit odd numbers. The first digit has 9 choices (1-9), the second digit has 10 choices, the third digit also has 10 choices, and the last digit must be odd (1, 3, 5, 7, or 9). So A equals $9(10)(10)(5)=4500$.\nNext, find B, the number of four-digit multiples of 5. Multiply the first digit (other than 0) by 9 for the actual digits. We have 5 possibilities for the first digit, so B equals $5(9)(9)(9)=4050$.\nTherefore, A+B equals $4500+4050=\\boxed{8550}$.\nThe answer is: 8550",
